# Data Visualization with ggplot2 {#sec-data-viz}



::: {.callout-note title="Learning Objectives"}
After completing this chapter, you will be able to:

- Explain the Grammar of Graphics philosophy underlying ggplot2
- Create common plot types: scatter plots, histograms, box plots, bar charts
- Customize plots with colors, labels, and themes
- Use faceting to create multi-panel figures
- Apply data visualization best practices
- Avoid common visualization pitfalls
:::

## Why Visualize Data? {#sec-why-visualize}

Before diving into the mechanics of creating plots, it's worth asking: why visualize data at all? Why not just report the numbers?

The answer lies in how our brains process information. The human visual system has evolved over millions of years to detect patterns, spot outliers, and perceive relationships. A well-designed visualization leverages this natural capability, allowing us to understand complex datasets almost instantly.

Consider the famous **Anscombe's Quartet**—four datasets that have nearly identical summary statistics (same mean, variance, and correlation) but tell completely different stories when plotted (@fig-anscombes):


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Anscombe's Quartet demonstration
anscombe_long <- anscombe |>
  pivot_longer(everything(),
               names_to = c(".value", "set"),
               names_pattern = "(.)(.)")

ggplot(anscombe_long, aes(x = x, y = y)) +
  geom_point(size = 2, color = "steelblue") +
  geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = FALSE, color = "coral") +
  facet_wrap(~set, ncol = 2) +
  labs(title = "Anscombe's Quartet",
       subtitle = "Same mean, variance, and correlation—different stories") +
  theme_minimal()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Anscombe's Quartet: Four datasets with identical summary statistics but very different patterns](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-anscombes-1.png){#fig-anscombes fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


This example powerfully demonstrates that summary statistics alone can hide important patterns. Visualization reveals what numbers cannot.

## The Grammar of Graphics {#sec-grammar-graphics}

**ggplot2** is R's most popular visualization package, built on the "Grammar of Graphics" framework [@wickham2016ggplot2]. Just as grammar provides rules for constructing sentences, the grammar of graphics provides rules for constructing statistical graphics.

### Seven Components of a Graphic {#sec-seven-components-of-a-graphic}

Every ggplot2 visualization is built from these components (@tbl-grammar and @fig-grammar-graphics):

| Component | Description | Example |
|:----------|:------------|:--------|
| **Data** | The dataset being visualized | `mtcars`, `iris` |
| **Aesthetics** | Mappings from data to visual properties | x, y, color, size, shape |
| **Geometries** | Visual elements representing data | points, lines, bars |
| **Facets** | Subplots for data subsets | panels by category |
| **Statistics** | Transformations of data | binning, smoothing |
| **Coordinates** | The coordinate system | Cartesian, polar |
| **Themes** | Non-data visual elements | fonts, backgrounds |

: Components of the Grammar of Graphics {#tbl-grammar}

![The Grammar of Graphics framework](images/visualization_grammar_of_graphics.jpeg){#fig-grammar-graphics}

## Building a Plot Layer by Layer {#sec-plot-layers}

ggplot2 builds plots incrementally using the `+` operator (@fig-basic-ggplot):


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Start with data and aesthetics
ggplot(data = mtcars, aes(x = wt, y = mpg)) +
  # Add a geometry layer
  geom_point()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![A minimal ggplot2 scatter plot of car weight against fuel efficiency](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-basic-ggplot-1.png){#fig-basic-ggplot fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


Let's break this down:

1. `ggplot()` initializes the plot with data
2. `aes()` maps variables to visual properties (aesthetics)
3. `geom_point()` adds the point geometry

### The Template {#sec-the-template}

Most ggplot2 code follows this template:

```r
ggplot(data = <DATA>, aes(<MAPPINGS>)) +
  <GEOM_FUNCTION>() +
  <OPTIONAL_LAYERS>
```

## Choosing the Right Chart Type {#sec-choose-chart}

One of the most important decisions in visualization is selecting the appropriate chart type for your data and message. Different charts excel at different tasks (@tbl-chart-types):

| Question | Chart Type | Why |
|:---------|:-----------|:----|
| How are values distributed? | Histogram, density plot | Shows shape, center, spread |
| How do groups compare? | Box plot, bar chart | Side-by-side comparison |
| How do two variables relate? | Scatter plot | Shows correlation, patterns |
| How does a value change over time? | Line plot | Connects sequential observations |
| What is the composition? | Stacked bar, pie chart | Shows parts of a whole |

: Matching questions to chart types {#tbl-chart-types}

::: {.callout-important title="Start with the Question"}
Before creating any visualization, ask yourself: "What question am I trying to answer?" The chart type should emerge from the question, not the other way around.
:::

## Essential Geoms {#sec-geoms}

### Scatter Plots with `geom_point()` {#sec-geom-point}

Scatter plots show relationships between two continuous variables:


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(mtcars, aes(x = wt, y = mpg)) +
  geom_point() +
  labs(
    title = "Fuel Efficiency vs. Weight",
    x = "Weight (1000 lbs)",
    y = "Miles per Gallon"
  )
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Relationship between car weight and fuel efficiency](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-scatter-1.png){#fig-scatter fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


### Adding Color and Shape {#sec-adding-color-and-shape}

Map additional variables to aesthetics:


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(mtcars, aes(x = wt, y = mpg, color = factor(cyl))) +
  geom_point(size = 3) +
  labs(
    title = "Fuel Efficiency by Weight and Cylinders",
    x = "Weight (1000 lbs)",
    y = "Miles per Gallon",
    color = "Cylinders"
  )
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Scatter plot with color representing cylinders](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-scatter-color-1.png){#fig-scatter-color fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


![Scatter plots are excellent for exploring relationships between two continuous variables. This example shows a positive correlation between two traits, demonstrating how scatter plots can reveal patterns in biological data.](images/visualization_scatter_correlation.jpeg){#fig-scatter-correlation width="60%"}

### Histograms with `geom_histogram()` {#sec-geom-histogram}

Histograms show the distribution of a single continuous variable:


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(diamonds, aes(x = price)) +
  geom_histogram(binwidth = 500, fill = "steelblue", color = "white") +
  labs(
    title = "Distribution of Diamond Prices",
    x = "Price (USD)",
    y = "Count"
  )
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Distribution of diamond prices](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-histogram-1.png){#fig-histogram fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


### Frequency Polygons with `geom_freqpoly()` {#sec-geom-freqpoly}

Frequency polygons are an alternative to histograms, showing counts as lines instead of bars. They're especially useful for comparing multiple distributions:


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(diamonds, aes(x = price, color = cut)) +
  geom_freqpoly(binwidth = 1000, linewidth = 1) +
  labs(
    title = "Price Distribution by Cut Quality",
    x = "Price (USD)",
    y = "Count"
  )
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Comparing distributions with frequency polygons](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-freqpoly-1.png){#fig-freqpoly fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


### Density Plots with `geom_density()` {#sec-geom-density}

Smoothed version of histograms:


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(diamonds, aes(x = price, fill = cut)) +
  geom_density(alpha = 0.5) +
  labs(
    title = "Price Distribution by Cut Quality",
    x = "Price (USD)",
    y = "Density"
  )
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Density plot comparing distributions by cut quality](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-density-1.png){#fig-density fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


### Box Plots with `geom_boxplot()` {#sec-geom-boxplot}

Box plots show distribution summaries (@fig-boxplot-anatomy) and are excellent for comparisons:


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(mtcars, aes(x = factor(cyl), y = mpg, fill = factor(cyl))) +
  geom_boxplot(show.legend = FALSE) +
  labs(
    title = "Fuel Efficiency by Cylinder Count",
    x = "Number of Cylinders",
    y = "Miles per Gallon"
  )
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Fuel efficiency by number of cylinders](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-boxplot-1.png){#fig-boxplot fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


![Anatomy of a box plot](images/visualization_boxplot_anatomy.jpeg){#fig-boxplot-anatomy width="60%"}

### Bar Charts with `geom_bar()` and `geom_col()` {#sec-geom-bar}

Use `geom_bar()` for counts and `geom_col()` for values:


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(diamonds, aes(x = cut, fill = cut)) +
  geom_bar(show.legend = FALSE) +
  labs(
    title = "Diamond Count by Cut Quality",
    x = "Cut",
    y = "Count"
  )
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Count of diamonds by cut quality](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-bar-1.png){#fig-bar fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


#### Grouped Bar Charts with `position = "dodge"`

When you have two categorical variables, use `position = "dodge"` to place bars side-by-side:


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(diamonds, aes(x = cut, fill = clarity)) +
  geom_bar(position = "dodge") +
  labs(
    title = "Diamond Count by Cut and Clarity",
    x = "Cut",
    y = "Count"
  )
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Grouped bar chart showing cut quality by clarity](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-bar-dodge-1.png){#fig-bar-dodge fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


#### Horizontal Bar Charts with `coord_flip()`

For long category names or many categories, flip the coordinates to make labels readable:


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(mpg, aes(x = class, fill = class)) +
  geom_bar(show.legend = FALSE) +
  coord_flip() +
  labs(
    title = "Vehicle Count by Class",
    x = NULL,
    y = "Count"
  )
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Horizontal bar chart for better label readability](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-bar-horizontal-1.png){#fig-bar-horizontal fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


### Line Plots with `geom_line()` {#sec-geom-line}

Line plots are ideal for time series or connected observations:


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Create sample time series data
set.seed(42)
time_data <- tibble(
  day = 1:30,
  value = cumsum(rnorm(30))
)

ggplot(time_data, aes(x = day, y = value)) +
  geom_line(color = "darkblue", linewidth = 1) +
  geom_point(color = "darkblue") +
  labs(
    title = "Simulated Time Series",
    x = "Day",
    y = "Value"
  )
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Example line plot](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-line-1.png){#fig-line fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


## Combining Geoms {#sec-combining-geoms}

Layer multiple geoms to create richer visualizations:


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(mtcars, aes(x = wt, y = mpg)) +
  geom_point(aes(color = factor(cyl)), size = 3) +
  geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = TRUE, color = "black") +
  labs(
    title = "Weight vs. MPG with Linear Trend",
    x = "Weight (1000 lbs)",
    y = "Miles per Gallon",
    color = "Cylinders"
  )
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Scatter plot with trend line](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-combined-1.png){#fig-combined fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


::: {.callout-warning title="Choose Appropriate Models"}
When adding trend lines or smoothing to your data, choose methods that match your data structure and research questions. Overfitting with overly complex models can obscure real patterns rather than reveal them (@fig-curve-fitting-humor).

![Different curve-fitting methods send different messages about your data. Choose methods that match your hypotheses and data characteristics, not just what makes the prettiest curve. Source: xkcd.com](images/visualization_curve_fitting.png){#fig-curve-fitting-humor width="70%"}
:::

## Faceting: Small Multiples {#sec-faceting}

Faceting creates separate panels for subsets of data.

### `facet_wrap()` for One Variable {#sec-facet-wrap}


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(mtcars, aes(x = wt, y = mpg)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = FALSE) +
  facet_wrap(~cyl) +
  labs(
    title = "Fuel Efficiency by Weight (Panels by Cylinders)",
    x = "Weight (1000 lbs)",
    y = "Miles per Gallon"
  )
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![MPG vs weight, faceted by cylinder count](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-facet-wrap-1.png){#fig-facet-wrap fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


### `facet_grid()` for Two Variables {#sec-facet-grid}


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(mtcars, aes(x = wt, y = mpg)) +
  geom_point() +
  facet_grid(am ~ cyl, labeller = labeller(
    am = c("0" = "Automatic", "1" = "Manual"),
    cyl = c("4" = "4 cyl", "6" = "6 cyl", "8" = "8 cyl")
  )) +
  labs(
    title = "MPG by Weight, Transmission, and Cylinders",
    x = "Weight (1000 lbs)",
    y = "Miles per Gallon"
  )
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Two-dimensional faceting](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-facet-grid-1.png){#fig-facet-grid fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


## Customizing Your Plots {#sec-plot-customization}

### Labels and Titles {#sec-labels-and-titles}


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(mtcars, aes(x = wt, y = mpg)) +
  geom_point() +
  labs(
    title = "Car Fuel Efficiency",
    subtitle = "Data from the 1974 Motor Trend magazine",
    x = "Weight (1000 lbs)",
    y = "Miles per Gallon",
    caption = "Source: mtcars dataset"
  )
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Plot with comprehensive labels](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-labels-1.png){#fig-labels fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


### Themes {#sec-themes}

ggplot2 includes several built-in themes:


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(patchwork)

p <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(x = wt, y = mpg)) +
  geom_point() +
  labs(title = "")

p1 <- p + theme_gray() + labs(title = "theme_gray (default)")
p2 <- p + theme_minimal() + labs(title = "theme_minimal")
p3 <- p + theme_classic() + labs(title = "theme_classic")
p4 <- p + theme_bw() + labs(title = "theme_bw")

(p1 + p2) / (p3 + p4)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Comparison of ggplot2 themes](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-themes-1.png){#fig-themes fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


### Color Scales {#sec-color-scales}


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(mtcars, aes(x = wt, y = mpg, color = factor(cyl))) +
  geom_point(size = 3) +
  scale_color_brewer(palette = "Set1") +
  labs(
    title = "Using Color Brewer Palette",
    color = "Cylinders"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Custom color palette](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-colors-1.png){#fig-colors fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


## Data Visualization Best Practices {#sec-viz-best-practices}

Good data visualization communicates clearly and honestly. Follow these principles:

### Show the Data {#sec-show-the-data}

Don't hide your data behind summaries when individual points are informative:


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(mtcars, aes(x = factor(cyl), y = mpg)) +
  geom_boxplot(outlier.shape = NA, fill = "lightblue") +
  geom_jitter(width = 0.2, alpha = 0.5) +
  labs(
    title = "Show the Data, Not Just the Summary",
    x = "Cylinders",
    y = "MPG"
  )
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Showing raw data with summary statistics](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-show-data-1.png){#fig-show-data fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


### Use Position and Length for Precision {#sec-use-position-and-length-for-precision}

Humans are best at comparing positions and lengths. Angles (pie charts) and areas are harder to judge:


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Create summary data
cut_summary <- diamonds |>
  count(cut) |>
  mutate(pct = n / sum(n) * 100)

ggplot(cut_summary, aes(x = reorder(cut, pct), y = pct, fill = cut)) +
  geom_col(show.legend = FALSE) +
  coord_flip() +
  labs(
    title = "Diamond Distribution by Cut",
    x = "Cut Quality",
    y = "Percentage"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Bar chart provides clearer comparison than a pie chart would](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-bar-vs-pie-1.png){#fig-bar-vs-pie fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


::: {.callout-warning title="Avoid Pie Charts"}
Pie charts make comparisons difficult because humans are poor at judging angles. Use bar charts instead for categorical comparisons.
:::

### Order by Meaningful Values {#sec-order-by-meaningful-values}

By default, R orders categorical variables alphabetically, which is rarely meaningful. Use `reorder()` to order by a numeric value instead (the forcats tools in @sec-reordering-factor-levels do the same job for factors):


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Calculate mean mpg by manufacturer
mtcars_summary <- mtcars |>
  mutate(car = rownames(mtcars)) |>
  slice_head(n = 10) |>
  mutate(car = reorder(car, mpg))  # Order car names by mpg values

ggplot(mtcars_summary, aes(x = car, y = mpg)) +
  geom_col(fill = "steelblue") +
  coord_flip() +
  labs(
    title = "Fuel Efficiency by Car (Ordered)",
    x = NULL,
    y = "Miles per Gallon"
  )
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Ordering categories by value aids comparison](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-ordered-1.png){#fig-ordered fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


The `reorder()` function takes a categorical variable and a numeric variable, then reorders the categories by the numeric values. You can also specify a summary function when there are multiple values per category (@fig-reorder-example):


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Order vehicle class by median highway mpg
ggplot(mpg, aes(x = reorder(class, hwy, FUN = median), y = hwy)) +
  geom_boxplot(fill = "lightblue") +
  coord_flip() +
  labs(
    title = "Highway MPG by Vehicle Class",
    subtitle = "Ordered by median MPG",
    x = NULL,
    y = "Highway MPG"
  )
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Highway MPG by vehicle class, with classes ordered by their median using `reorder()`](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-reorder-example-1.png){#fig-reorder-example fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


### Include Zero for Bar Charts {#sec-include-zero-for-bar-charts}

When using bars, start the y-axis at zero. Otherwise, you distort relative magnitudes:


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Example data
sales <- tibble(
  quarter = c("Q1", "Q2", "Q3", "Q4"),
  revenue = c(100, 105, 110, 108)
)

ggplot(sales, aes(x = quarter, y = revenue)) +
  geom_col(fill = "coral") +
  labs(
    title = "Quarterly Revenue (Starting at Zero)",
    x = "Quarter",
    y = "Revenue"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Bar charts should include zero to show true proportions](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-include-zero-1.png){#fig-include-zero fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


### Consider Color Blindness {#sec-consider-color-blindness}

About 8% of men and 0.5% of women have color vision deficiency. Use colorblind-friendly palettes:


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Colorblind-friendly colors
cb_palette <- c("#E69F00", "#56B4E9", "#009E73", "#F0E442",
                "#0072B2", "#D55E00", "#CC79A7")

ggplot(mtcars, aes(x = wt, y = mpg, color = factor(cyl))) +
  geom_point(size = 3) +
  scale_color_manual(values = cb_palette) +
  labs(
    title = "Using a Colorblind-Friendly Palette",
    color = "Cylinders"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Colorblind-friendly palette](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-colorblind-1.png){#fig-colorblind fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


::: {.callout-tip}
The viridis color scales (`scale_color_viridis_d()`, `scale_fill_viridis_c()`) are designed to be colorblind-friendly and perceptually uniform.
:::

### Avoid Chart Junk {#sec-avoid-chart-junk}

Remove unnecessary visual elements that don't convey information:

- Avoid 3D effects
- Remove unnecessary gridlines
- Don't use gradient fills for categorical data
- Skip decorative elements

## Common Visualization Tasks {#sec-common-viz-tasks}

### Comparing Distributions {#sec-comparing-distributions}


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(iris, aes(x = Sepal.Length, fill = Species)) +
  geom_density(alpha = 0.6) +
  scale_fill_brewer(palette = "Set2") +
  labs(
    title = "Sepal Length Distribution by Species",
    x = "Sepal Length (cm)",
    y = "Density"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Comparing distributions with density plots](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-distributions-1.png){#fig-distributions fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


### Before-After Comparisons {#sec-before-after-comparisons}

Slope charts effectively show changes between two time points:


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Sample before-after data
change_data <- tibble(
  subject = rep(LETTERS[1:5], 2),
  time = rep(c("Before", "After"), each = 5),
  value = c(10, 12, 8, 15, 11, 14, 15, 11, 18, 13)
)

ggplot(change_data, aes(x = time, y = value, group = subject)) +
  geom_line(aes(color = subject), linewidth = 1) +
  geom_point(aes(color = subject), size = 3) +
  labs(
    title = "Before-After Comparison",
    x = NULL,
    y = "Value"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Slope chart showing change over time](09-data-visualization_files/figure-html/fig-slope-1.png){#fig-slope fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::


## Saving Plots {#sec-saving-plots}

Use `ggsave()` to export plots; in a Quarto document, figures are instead saved automatically at render time (@sec-figure-options):


::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Create a plot
p <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(x = wt, y = mpg)) +
  geom_point() +
  theme_minimal()

# Save as PNG (good for web)
ggsave("my_plot.png", plot = p, width = 8, height = 6, dpi = 300)

# Save as PDF (good for publications)
ggsave("my_plot.pdf", plot = p, width = 8, height = 6)

# Save as SVG (good for editing)
ggsave("my_plot.svg", plot = p, width = 8, height = 6)
```
:::


## Summary {#sec-viz-summary}

ggplot2 provides a powerful and consistent framework for data visualization:

- Build plots layer by layer using the Grammar of Graphics
- Map data to aesthetics (x, y, color, size, shape)
- Add geometries to represent data visually
- Use faceting to create multi-panel figures
- Customize with themes, labels, and color scales

Best practices for effective visualization:

- Show the data when possible
- Use position and length over angles and area
- Order categories meaningfully
- Include zero for bar charts
- Use colorblind-friendly palettes
- Avoid chart junk and 3D effects

